description:
Right now whenever you want to structure a generic it informs you to register a hook, every time you change the type in that generic you need to register a new hook. What this PR addresses is supporting generic structure of attrs classes based on the typing information supplied.

```
from attr import attrs, attrib, make_class
from typing import Dict, Type, Optional, List, TypeVar, Generic
from cattr import structure

@attrs(auto_attribs=True)
class M(Generic[Q]):
    m: Q

@attrs(auto_attribs=True)
class N(Generic[P]):
    n: P

data2 = {
    "n": {
        "m": "test"
    }
}

a = structure(data2, N[M[str]])
print(a)
```

This allows for deeply nested types and handling generics properly based on the type entered at the structure level. It handles duplicate Type variable names like `T` being used everywhere in the code base, and it supports generics in every sub structure method I have been able to test (unions, list, sets, etc.) to build up an attrs class.

The only issue with this PR is 2 things:
* This is a breaking change, in order for this to work we need to have the mapping information of what the type `T~` actually is suppose to map too, so all structure hooks need to accept 3 variables instead of 2
* All current tests are passing, but I have absolutely zero idea about how to make hypotheses generate random classes and their types to test structuring. I was attempting to look at the nested_classes strategy but I am not familiar enough with hypothesis to get this going.

If some static tests are acceptable, I can easily add those in but I will need some assistance sorting out the hypothesis portion to get tests in line with the current code base.
